(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-pd1-exam2-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"PD1 Exam 2 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form D","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Palpating any abnormal areas noted on inspection (per the exam sequence) should occur...","choices":["only if the patient specifically requests that step","before any visual inspection has even begun to occur","after visual inspection has identified the area of concern","only during the separate digital prostate portion of the exam"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sequence is inspect first, then palpate any abnormal areas identified — palpation is targeted based on what inspection reveals, not performed blindly before looking. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"Reviewing a younger patient's sexual maturity rating during the GU (genitourinary) exam serves mainly to...","choices":["assess entirely unrelated cardiac function through auscultation of heart sounds directly","determine the patient's blood type through a rapid bedside antigen agglutination test","accurately document pubertal developmental stage and distinguish normal variation from abnormal findings","entirely replace the separate need for performing the rest of the physical examination"],"answer":2,"exp":"Documenting sexual maturity rating provides a standardized way to record a younger patient's pubertal developmental stage, helping distinguish normal developmental variation from a genuinely abnormal finding. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"Deep palpation of the abdomen, performed with two hands using the palmar surfaces of the fingers, presses to a depth of approximately...","choices":["10 cm or more","3 to 4 cm","deep palpation uses no measurable depth target","less than 1 cm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Deep palpation presses to a depth of about 3 to 4 cm using the palmar surfaces of the fingers, typically bimanually, in all four quadrants, to better assess for masses or organomegaly beyond what light palpation can detect. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 37"},{"q":"Increased voice transmission findings (bronchophony, whispered pectoriloquy) occur in situations where...","choices":["the lung is hyperinflated with chronic air trapping from emphysema","a large pneumothorax has caused collapse of the underlying lung tissue","normal vesicular breath sounds are heard throughout both lung fields","bronchial breathing is heard, making the sounds loud, sharp, and distinct"],"answer":3,"exp":"Increased voice transmission occurs any time bronchial-type breath sounds are present over an area that should normally have vesicular sounds, making transmitted voice sounds loud, sharp, and distinct rather than muffled — a sign of consolidation. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 53"},{"q":"On skin inspection, the examiner assesses color, uniformity, thickness, symmetry, hygiene, lesions, scars, rashes, dryness, and...","choices":["bowel sounds","heart rate","odors","blood pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"The skin inspection checklist includes color, uniformity, thickness, symmetry, hygiene, lesions, scars, rashes, dryness, and odors. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"The consensual pupillary reaction refers to...","choices":["dilation observed in the opposite eye when light is shone into one eye","constriction observed in the same eye that is being directly illuminated","a pupillary test entirely unrelated to any light exposure or stimulus","constriction observed in the opposite eye when light is shone into one eye"],"answer":3,"exp":"The consensual reaction is pupillary constriction observed in the eye opposite to the one being directly illuminated, reflecting the bilateral neural pathway of the pupillary light reflex; this is best elicited with a hand placed across the bridge of the nose to prevent light from directly reaching the opposite eye. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"When assessing respirations, the examiner observes rate, rhythm, depth, and...","choices":["visual acuity","effort","pupil size","hearing threshold"],"answer":1,"exp":"A complete respiratory assessment includes rate, rhythm, depth, and effort (for example, use of accessory muscles, labored appearance), observed visually and, as needed, with the stethoscope. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"Arterioles are best described as:","choices":["Thin-walled exchange vessels with only an endothelial lining, lacking smooth muscle in their walls","Thin-walled channels that transport lymph fluid rather than blood back toward the venous system","Large, highly elastic conduit vessels, like the aorta, that buffer pulsatile arterial pressure","Resistance vessels whose smooth muscle tone significantly determines systemic vascular resistance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Arterioles are resistance vessels, and their smooth muscle tone is a major determinant of systemic vascular resistance. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 33"},{"q":"When inspecting the pharyngeal wall, which findings should be assessed?","choices":["Post-nasal drip, exudate, and erythema","Only the color of the hard palate","Only the size of the visible tonsils","Only the quality of the patient's voice"],"answer":0,"exp":"These are the specific pharyngeal wall inspection findings listed in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 51"},{"q":"A history of chronic constipation and straining is relevant to the anorectal exam because it is a recognized risk factor for...","choices":["hearing loss","hemorrhoids and anal fissures","prostate cancer exclusively","no anorectal conditions at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Chronic straining (as with constipation) increases pressure in the anal canal, a well-recognized risk factor for developing both hemorrhoids and anal fissures. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"A firm, non-tender nodule palpated on the testis itself (not the epididymis) should raise concern for...","choices":["benign prostatic hyperplasia, a prostate condition","testicular cancer, warranting further urgent workup","a normal finding requiring no further workup","a normal variant seen commonly in most healthy men"],"answer":1,"exp":"A firm, painless nodule on the testis itself is a classic concerning finding for testicular cancer and should prompt urgent further evaluation (for example, ultrasound), unlike epididymal findings, which are more often benign/infectious/cystic in nature. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"When palpating the liver using the standard bimanual technique, the examiner's left hand is used to...","choices":["percuss the liver span","press directly on the right upper quadrant to feel for the liver edge","auscultate for bowel sounds","lift gently under the patient's right flank"],"answer":3,"exp":"In the standard bimanual liver palpation technique, the left hand is placed under the patient's right flank and gently lifts upward, while the right hand palpates the abdomen searching for the liver edge as the patient breathes deeply. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 40"},{"q":"A quantitative decrease in voice transmission (decreased fremitus/resonance) could be due to...","choices":["normal, healthy lung tissue in a well-conditioned adult with no cardiopulmonary disease","any form of lung or pleural disease that impairs sound conduction, such as pleural effusion or pneumothorax","consolidation from lobar pneumonia exclusively, which instead increases transmission","egophony, a qualitative E-to-A voice change, occurring exclusively over consolidated lung"],"answer":1,"exp":"A quantitative decrease in voice transmission can result from any condition that impairs sound conduction from the lung to the chest wall, such as pleural effusion, pneumothorax, or other pleural/lung disease creating an air or fluid barrier. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 54"},{"q":"On skin palpation, the examiner assesses moisture, texture, and temperature; temperature is best assessed using...","choices":["a stethoscope","visual inspection alone, without touching the patient","the palmar surface of the hands exclusively","the dorsal surface of the examiner's hands"],"answer":3,"exp":"Skin temperature is best assessed using the dorsal (back) surface of the examiner's hands, which is more sensitive to temperature differences, checking for bilateral symmetry. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 19"},{"q":"Testing near reaction, which assesses pupillary constriction with near effort and convergence, is performed specifically...","choices":["if the reaction to light is impaired or questionable","only in patients under age 10","only using the ophthalmoscope","as the very first pupillary test, before light reaction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Near reaction testing is performed specifically when the pupillary light reaction is impaired or gives a questionable result, helping further characterize the pupillary response. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 26"},{"q":"Blood pressure represents a peripheral measurement of cardiovascular function; pulse pressure is calculated as...","choices":["systolic minus diastolic pressure","diastolic minus systolic pressure","systolic plus diastolic pressure","systolic divided by diastolic pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pulse pressure is calculated as systolic blood pressure minus diastolic blood pressure, reflecting the difference between the peak and trough arterial pressures during the cardiac cycle. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which of the following should be asked about when taking a peripheral vascular history?","choices":["Bowel habits, stool consistency, and abdominal symptoms","Visual acuity, eye pain, and history of eye trauma","Skin color, temperature, and symptoms provoked by exertion","Hearing loss, tinnitus, and history of ear infections"],"answer":2,"exp":"These are specifically listed elements to ask about when assessing peripheral vascular history. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 38"},{"q":"On neck inspection, which findings should be assessed?","choices":["Only the presence or absence of noticeable hoarseness in the patient's speaking voice","Only the patient's overall active and passive cervical range of motion","Only the quality and amplitude of the bilateral carotid pulses on palpation","Symmetry, masses, scars, gland enlargement, visible lymph nodes, and tracheal position"],"answer":3,"exp":"These are the comprehensive neck inspection findings listed, including trachea midline versus deviation and thyroid symmetry/masses/rise with swallowing. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 60"},{"q":"A rectovaginal exam, one of the special maneuvers listed, is particularly useful for assessing...","choices":["the rectovaginal septum and posterior pelvic structures not reachable by a rectal exam alone","lung sounds, assessed separately through routine pulmonary auscultation technique","visual acuity, assessed separately through a formal ophthalmologic screening exam","thyroid size, assessed separately through careful anterior neck palpation technique"],"answer":0,"exp":"The rectovaginal exam (one finger in the rectum, one in the vagina) allows assessment of the rectovaginal septum and posterior pelvic structures (like the uterosacral ligaments or posterior uterus/adnexa) that a rectal exam alone cannot fully evaluate. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"Scrotal or testicular pain, swelling, or lesions, as a screened history item, is important to ask about because it can indicate conditions ranging from...","choices":["exclusively cardiac conditions, since scrotal vessels are said to share the heart's blood supply","only minor, entirely self-limited issues that never require any urgent clinical attention","benign cysts to urgent conditions like torsion, requiring careful triage based on onset and severity","exclusively dermatologic conditions, confined strictly to the surface of the scrotal skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Scrotal/testicular symptoms span a wide range of causes — from relatively benign findings like epididymal cysts to time-sensitive emergencies like testicular torsion — making careful history-taking about onset, severity, and associated symptoms essential for appropriate triage. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"During bimanual liver palpation, the patient is asked to take a deep breath while the examiner...","choices":["asks the patient to hold their breath firmly in place before any palpation of the abdomen even begins","releases all applied pressure entirely, removing the hand from skin contact completely and fully","presses gently inward and upward with the right hand, feeling for the liver edge as it descends with inspiration","moves the right hand entirely away from the right side, over to the left upper quadrant instead"],"answer":2,"exp":"As the diaphragm descends with a deep breath, the liver moves downward; the examiner presses gently inward and upward with the right hand at that moment to feel the liver edge slide against the fingertips. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 40"},{"q":"Among the normal findings summarized for palpation of the chest are a midline trachea, no tenderness, and...","choices":["significant tenderness bilaterally","symmetric, unaccentuated tactile fremitus","a deviated trachea toward one side","asymmetric, markedly increased fremitus on one side"],"answer":1,"exp":"Normal palpation findings include a midline trachea, absence of tenderness, and tactile fremitus that is symmetric and not accentuated (increased) on either side. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 58"},{"q":"Skin turgor, assessed on palpation, refers to...","choices":["the overall color pattern and pigmentation seen across the skin surface","the overall presence and distribution of any visible skin lesions found","the overall pattern and density of body hair distribution observed","the fullness or tension produced by the fluid content of cells and tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Turgor reflects the fullness/tension of the skin from its fluid content; normal skin should feel resilient, move easily when gently pinched, and return to place immediately when released. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"During near reaction testing, the examiner holds a finger approximately how far from the patient's eye, having them alternate looking at the finger and into the distance?","choices":["there is no specific distance used","1 meter","10 cm","50 cm"],"answer":2,"exp":"The examiner holds a finger approximately 10 cm from the patient's eye during near reaction testing, asking the patient to alternate looking at the finger and looking into the distance past it; the pupils should constrict with near effort and convergence. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 26"},{"q":"Regarding blood pressure cuff size, using a cuff that is too small for the patient's upper arm circumference will produce a reading that is...","choices":["exactly accurate regardless of size mismatch","artificially low","artificially high","unaffected by cuff size"],"answer":2,"exp":"A cuff that is too small for the arm circumference will produce a falsely elevated (artificially high) blood pressure reading, since more external pressure is needed to compress the artery through the narrower effective cuff coverage. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 48"},{"q":"During inspection of the lower extremities, what must be ensured regarding patient positioning and privacy?","choices":["No draping is required as long as the exam room door remains closed","The patient is supine and appropriately draped, with external genitalia covered","The patient remains fully clothed throughout the entire inspection","The patient stands completely unclothed for optimal visualization"],"answer":1,"exp":"Lower extremity inspection requires supine positioning with appropriate draping and coverage of the external genitalia. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 40"},{"q":"When palpating the trachea for position, what technique is used?","choices":["No physical bedside technique exists for this at all; tracheal position can only be reliably assessed using chest imaging studies","Stand directly behind the seated patient and use both thumbs simultaneously to press firmly on either side of the trachea near the sternal notch","Stand in front of the patient, place an index finger on each side of the trachea, and evaluate the distance to the sternocleidomastoid in several locations, which should be symmetrical","Ask the patient to swallow repeatedly while the examiner auscultates directly over the tracheal air column with the stethoscope bell"],"answer":2,"exp":"This is the correct technique described for assessing tracheal position/midline status. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 61"},{"q":"A thrombosed external hemorrhoid classically presents as...","choices":["a normal finding in most adults","diffuse perianal erythema with no discrete lump","a sudden, painful, bluish, tender perianal lump","a painless, slowly enlarging mass"],"answer":2,"exp":"A thrombosed external hemorrhoid presents as an acutely painful, tender, bluish (from clotted blood) lump at the anal verge, often developing suddenly. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"Because prostate cancer is often asymptomatic in its early stages, the health promotion emphasis for this condition centers on...","choices":["treating every single patient completely identically, regardless of their individual age or family risk factors","appropriate screening discussions (for example, PSA (prostate-specific antigen) testing) rather than waiting for symptoms to appear","relying solely on patient self-examination performed at home, exactly as taught for testicular self-exams","simply ignoring the condition entirely until noticeable symptoms eventually happen to clearly develop"],"answer":1,"exp":"Since early prostate cancer often produces no symptoms, health promotion focuses on informed screening discussions (such as PSA (prostate-specific antigen) testing) tailored to patient risk factors, rather than waiting for symptomatic presentation. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"If the liver edge is not felt on the first attempt at its expected location, the examiner should...","choices":["stop the entire abdominal exam immediately and completely, without attempting any further palpation maneuvers","immediately and confidently conclude that the liver is congenitally absent from the abdomen entirely","move the right hand upward and repeat, continuing until the liver edge is felt or the right costal margin is reached","move the palpating hand over to the left side of the abdomen instead, searching there for the edge"],"answer":2,"exp":"If the liver edge isn't felt initially, the examiner systematically moves the palpating hand upward and repeats the maneuver, continuing until either the liver edge is felt or the right costal margin is reached. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 41"},{"q":"Among the normal findings summarized for percussion of the chest are resonant, symmetric percussion notes and a diaphragmatic excursion range of...","choices":["dull, asymmetric percussion notes","3 to 5 cm","15 to 20 cm","0 cm (no measurable excursion)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The normal-findings summary specifies resonant, symmetric percussion notes throughout the lung fields and a diaphragmatic excursion in the 3-5 cm range (consistent with the earlier taught normal range). — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 59"},{"q":"Normal skin mobility, assessed alongside turgor, means the skin should...","choices":["remain tented/raised for an extended period after being pinched, unlike normal mobile skin","move easily when pinched and return to place immediately when released","only ever be assessed visually, and never through direct touch","remain completely rigid and immobile whenever it is gently pinched"],"answer":1,"exp":"Normal skin should be resilient and mobile — moving easily when gently pinched and snapping back to its original position immediately upon release; delayed return (tenting) can suggest dehydration or decreased turgor. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"To assess ocular alignment using corneal light reflection, the examiner stands approximately how far in front of the patient?","choices":["2 feet","6 inches","10 feet","there is no specific distance for this technique"],"answer":0,"exp":"The examiner stands approximately 2 feet in front of the patient, shines a light, and has the patient look at it; in normal alignment, the corneal light reflection should appear slightly medial of the pupil center in each eye. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"Using a blood pressure cuff that is too large for the patient's upper arm circumference will produce a reading that is...","choices":["exactly accurate regardless of size mismatch","artificially low","unaffected by cuff size","artificially high"],"answer":1,"exp":"A cuff that is too large for the arm circumference will produce a falsely low (artificially low) blood pressure reading, the opposite effect of an undersized cuff, which produces artificially high readings. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 48"},{"q":"When inspecting the extremities, which features should be noted?","choices":["Only the presence or absence of a gross deficit in manual muscle strength testing, with nothing else recorded","Only whether a palpable pulse can be felt at each of the standard peripheral pulse points","Only the patient's self-reported pain intensity rated on a numeric 1 to 10 pain scale","Size, symmetry, swelling, venous pattern, skin color/texture, temperature, hair growth, ulcerations, and nail beds"],"answer":3,"exp":"All of these features should be systematically noted during extremity inspection. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 40"},{"q":"When inspecting the thyroid as part of neck inspection, what maneuver helps assess it?","choices":["Ask the patient to turn their head fully to one side only, then observe the gland","Ask the patient to hold their breath and observe for jugular venous distension","Observe for symmetry, masses, and rise/fall of the gland while the patient swallows","No functional maneuver is needed here; only static visual inspection is performed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Asking the patient to swallow while observing the thyroid helps assess symmetry, masses, and normal gland movement. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 60"},{"q":"Watching the patient's face during the anorectal exam is a recommended practice mainly to...","choices":["gauge discomfort/pain in real time and adjust technique accordingly","formally assess the patient's visual acuity and eye movements","check specifically for signs of jaundice in the sclera","completely replace the ongoing need to ask about pain verbally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Observing facial expressions provides real-time feedback about patient discomfort or pain, allowing the examiner to adjust pressure/technique or pause as needed for patient comfort. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"A spermatocele, a possible finding on scrotal exam, is best described as...","choices":["a solid, non-cystic mass that arises directly from the substance of the testis itself and grows","a painless, sperm-containing cystic mass typically located above/behind the testis, near the epididymis","identical to a varicocele in both its diffuse texture and its typical location above the testis","always a definitive sign of testicular malignancy requiring immediate surgical intervention"],"answer":1,"exp":"A spermatocele is a painless, fluid/sperm-filled cystic mass usually found above or behind the testis in association with the epididymis, generally a benign finding distinct from the testis itself. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"The 'hooking technique' for liver palpation involves the examiner...","choices":["standing to the patient's left side, facing directly toward the patient's head the entire time","pressing straight downward firmly on the abdomen without ever hooking the fingers underneath the ribs","using only a single hand throughout the entire maneuver, with the other hand never involved at all","standing to the right, facing the patient's feet, and hooking both hands' fingers under the right costal margin"],"answer":3,"exp":"In the hooking technique, the examiner stands to the patient's right, facing toward the patient's feet, and hooks the fingers of both hands under the right costal margin, asking the patient to take a deep breath while palpating for the liver edge. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 43"},{"q":"Among the normal findings summarized for auscultation of the chest are an absence of adventitious sounds and vesicular sounds throughout, except...","choices":["everywhere in the chest, with absolutely no exceptions to this pattern","over the abdomen, where bronchial breath sounds are normally heard","near the sternal borders, where bronchovesicular sounds are normally heard","at the lung apices exclusively, where tracheal sounds predominate"],"answer":2,"exp":"While most of the peripheral lung fields normally have vesicular breath sounds, bronchovesicular sounds are normally heard near the sternal borders/central airways, reflecting the transition zone near the larger airways. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 59"},{"q":"On nail inspection, the examiner evaluates pigmentation, length, symmetry, cleanliness, condition of the free edge/cuticle, and...","choices":["the patient's resting blood pressure","bilateral lung sounds on exam","active bowel sounds present","ridging, beading, pitting, or peeling"],"answer":3,"exp":"The nail inspection checklist includes ridging, beading, pitting, and peeling, along with pigmentation, length, symmetry, and condition of the free edge and cuticle. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"In normal ocular alignment, the corneal light reflection should appear...","choices":["far lateral to the pupil, near the outer canthus","slightly medial of the pupil center","directly over the iris periphery, never near the pupil","absent entirely in normal eyes"],"answer":1,"exp":"With normal ocular alignment, the light reflection on the cornea should appear slightly medial to the center of the pupil in both eyes; asymmetry between the eyes suggests ocular misalignment (strabismus). — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"Proper blood pressure cuff position places the lower cuff border approximately how far above the antecubital crease?","choices":["there is no specific recommended cuff position","2.5 cm","15 cm","0 cm, directly touching the antecubital crease"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lower border of the BP (blood pressure) cuff should be positioned approximately 2.5 cm above the antecubital crease, with the cuff's marker aligned over the brachial artery to properly center the bladder. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 48"},{"q":"Where should the radial pulse be palpated?","choices":["Behind and below the medial malleolus, using the finger pads against the tibia","On the extensor surface of the dorsal wrist, just distal to the radius","Just medial to the biceps tendon at the antecubital crease, with the elbow slightly flexed","With the finger pads on the flexor surface of the lateral wrist, with the wrist partially flexed"],"answer":3,"exp":"The radial pulse is palpated with finger pads on the flexor surface of the lateral wrist; partial wrist flexion may aid palpation. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 43"},{"q":"During anterior thyroid palpation, what is the recommended technique?","choices":["Stand behind the seated patient and use only the left hand to palpate the entire thyroid gland","Use a wooden tongue blade to gently press on the visible thyroid gland from outside the neck","Stand facing the patient and palpate the thyroid gland outline and isthmus to confirm inspection findings","Palpate the gland only with the patient's neck held fully extended backward the entire time"],"answer":2,"exp":"The anterior approach involves facing the patient and palpating the outline/isthmus to confirm what was seen on inspection. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 62"},{"q":"Bright red blood noted only on the toilet paper (not mixed in the stool) after a bowel movement most classically points toward...","choices":["a pulmonary source, unrelated to the digestive tract","an upper GI (gastrointestinal) bleed such as a peptic ulcer","a normal finding requiring no further clinical evaluation","a distal source such as hemorrhoids or an anal fissure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Blood that appears only on wiping (toilet paper), rather than mixed throughout the stool, most classically points to a distal (anal/perianal) source such as hemorrhoids or a fissure, rather than a more proximal GI (gastrointestinal) bleed. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"Having an assistant/chaperone present during the male genital exam, when appropriate, primarily serves to...","choices":["is legally required for every single patient, regardless of their stated personal preference","provide additional patient comfort and support appropriate examination practice standards","is never considered appropriate at any point during this particular type of intimate exam","entirely replaces the examiner's separate need to fully explain the exam process beforehand"],"answer":1,"exp":"A chaperone can help the patient feel more comfortable during a sensitive exam and supports appropriate practice standards for conducting intimate examinations professionally. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"A key teaching point about the normal spleen on physical exam is that it is...","choices":["percussed using the midclavicular line, like the liver","located in the right upper quadrant","always easily palpable in every patient","usually not palpable"],"answer":3,"exp":"In a normal, healthy adult, the spleen is usually not palpable; feeling a spleen tip generally raises suspicion for splenomegaly. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 45"},{"q":"Tactile fremitus, assessed during chest palpation, refers to...","choices":["the patient's respiratory rate, counted by observing chest wall movement over a full sixty-second period","the palpable vibration felt on the chest wall as the patient speaks (for example, says 'ninety-nine')","a percussion note produced by striking the pleximeter finger with the plexor, heard without any palpation","a visual inspection finding, noted simply by observing the chest wall's movement and symmetry during breathing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Tactile fremitus is the vibration felt by the examiner's hands on the chest wall as the patient speaks, reflecting how well the underlying lung tissue transmits vocal vibrations — increased over consolidation, decreased over effusion/pneumothorax/hyperinflation. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 34"},{"q":"When palpating the nail plate, the examiner assesses texture, firmness, thickness, uniformity, and...","choices":["resting blood pressure reading","adherence to the nail bed","chest wall expansion pattern","bowel sound character and pitch"],"answer":1,"exp":"Nail plate palpation specifically assesses texture, firmness, thickness, uniformity, and adherence to the underlying nail bed, checking for any abnormal separation (onycholysis). — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"When assessing extraocular movements in the six cardinal directions of gaze using the 'H pattern' technique, the examiner should pause briefly at the far right, up, and down points specifically to detect...","choices":["visual acuity deficits","pupil size","color vision deficits","nystagmus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Briefly pausing at the extreme positions of gaze (far right, up, down, and similarly on the left) allows the examiner to observe for nystagmus (rhythmic involuntary eye oscillation), which may only become apparent at the extremes of gaze. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"For accurate blood pressure measurement, proper patient positioning includes being seated with back supported, feet on the ground with legs uncrossed, and having rested for approximately how long before measurement?","choices":["no rest period is needed","30 seconds","2 hours","5 minutes"],"answer":3,"exp":"The patient should rest for approximately 5 minutes before blood pressure measurement, seated with back supported and feet flat on the ground with legs uncrossed, and should avoid smoking or caffeinated beverages for 30 minutes prior. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 49"},{"q":"Where is the brachial pulse palpated?","choices":["On the flexor surface of the lateral wrist, with the wrist partially flexed","Just medial to the biceps tendon at the antecubital crease, with the elbow slightly flexed","Midway between the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic symphysis, below the inguinal ligament","Lateral to the extensor tendon of the great toe, on the dorsum of the foot"],"answer":1,"exp":"The brachial pulse is felt just medial to the biceps tendon at the antecubital crease with the elbow slightly flexed. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"In the posterior thyroid palpation technique, how is the right lobe palpated?","choices":["Palpate the right lobe with the right hand while displacing the trachea to the right using the left hand","Palpate the right lobe with the left hand while displacing the trachea to the left instead","Palpate both lobes simultaneously with both hands, without displacing the trachea at all","Stand in front of the patient directly, rather than behind, for this specific maneuver"],"answer":0,"exp":"This is the correct posterior technique: right hand palpates the right lobe while the left hand displaces the trachea toward that side. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 63"},{"q":"A normal, healthy prostate on DRE (digital rectal examination) should feel...","choices":["stony-hard in consistency, with multiple palpable irregular nodules","smooth, symmetric, and rubbery/firm (like the tip of the nose), non-tender","boggy, soft in texture, and exquisitely tender throughout to palpation","not palpable at all through the anterior rectal wall on exam"],"answer":1,"exp":"A normal prostate should feel smooth-surfaced, symmetric between its two lobes, and have a firm-but-rubbery consistency (often likened to the tip of the nose), without tenderness or nodularity. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"Inspecting the scrotum as part of the exam includes assessing its...","choices":["size, symmetry, and any visible skin changes or masses","near and distance visual acuity testing bilaterally","resting heart rate and pulse rhythm regularity","lung sounds heard bilaterally on chest auscultation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Scrotal inspection assesses overall size, symmetry between the two sides, and any visible skin changes (rashes, lesions) or masses, providing the initial visual screen before palpation. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"During spleen palpation, the examiner tries to feel the tip of the spleen touch the fingertips specifically...","choices":["during exhalation only, as the spleen ascends with the diaphragm","it cannot ever be felt on physical exam under any circumstances","during inhalation, as the spleen descends with the diaphragm","only when the patient is standing fully upright throughout"],"answer":2,"exp":"Similar to liver palpation, the spleen (when enlarged enough to be palpable) is felt touching the examiner's fingertips during inhalation, as it descends along with diaphragmatic movement. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 45"},{"q":"Besides tenderness and tactile fremitus, chest palpation also assesses for pulsations, bulges, depressions, crepitus, tracheal position, and...","choices":["symmetry of eye movements","displacement of mammary tissue","frequency of bowel sounds heard","pure-tone hearing thresholds"],"answer":1,"exp":"The palpation checklist specifically includes assessing for displacement of mammary tissue, alongside tenderness, pulsations, bulges, depressions, crepitus, respiratory excursion, tactile fremitus, and tracheal position. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 34"},{"q":"The normal nail base angle, formed by the proximal nail fold and nail plate, should measure approximately...","choices":["360 degrees","30 degrees","160 degrees","0 degrees (flat, with no angle)"],"answer":2,"exp":"The normal nail base angle (Lovibond angle), formed between the proximal nail fold and the nail plate, should measure approximately 160 degrees; an increased angle (approaching or exceeding 180 degrees) can be a sign of clubbing. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"During the H-pattern extraocular movement test, the examiner should hold the patient's head still and instructs them to...","choices":["follow the examiner's finger through the six cardinal positions of gaze, without pausing in the middle","look straight ahead the entire time without ever moving their eyes to track anything","keep their eyes closed completely throughout the entire six-position cardinal gaze test","move their head to actively track the finger, rather than moving just their eyes alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"The patient follows the examiner's finger through the six cardinal gaze positions in an H pattern while keeping the head still; the examiner specifically does not pause in the middle transition points, only pausing briefly at the far right/up/down and far left/up/down extremes to check for nystagmus. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 28"}]};
